From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:59:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Let the media reader of a paused element idle outside
 a document

A media element told the media cache that its reader was paused when
the element was paused or its document was not fully active, which kept
loading up to the read-ahead of the cache without a rate: the whole of
a 150 MB file for an element that was removed from its document and
paused, which nothing might ever resume.

The cache now distinguishes a paused reader, which loads ahead at the
rate of the resource so that playback can resume without waiting, from
an idle one, which loads nothing ahead and gives up its fetches. Tell
the reader of a paused element in a document that it is paused, with
the rate of the resource once its length and duration are known, and
the reader of an element whose document is not fully active, or that is
paused outside a document, that it idles. An element updates its reader
when it is inserted into or removed from a document as well.

The element also pushes data into a player whose reader idles only
until HAVE_ENOUGH_DATA and while it seeks, so that an element that was
never inserted still gets its metadata and first frame and completes
seeks: every block it read for the 50 MiB buffer of GStreamer made the
cache fetch for the idle reader again, 822 suspend events for the
150 MB file at 5 MB/s. It continues once the reader no longer idles.

Pushes into a player for a reader of the metadata are held the same way
once the player has the first frame (HAVE_CURRENT_DATA), unless it
seeks, since GStreamer otherwise asks for, and so loads, the whole
resource for preload=metadata: about 17 MB of a 150 MB file instead of
149.7 MB at 5 and 50 MB/s.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: no WPT test measures what a paused element downloads; see
Validation.

Validation: a 150 MB, 40 s file at 5 MB/s, paused in its document after 3 s for
40 s, 1 request and 149.7 MB (phase 1: 2 requests, 152.8 MB); removed
from its document and paused after 6 s, 33.2 MB and the connection
closed 30 s later, when the cache gives up the suspended fetch (phase 1:
86.5 MB, closed after 17 s; 89.5 MB, 822 suspend events and closed
after 47 s without the push gating); removed after 6 s and put back and played
after 30 s, it plays to the end in 1 request (phase 1: 2 requests). `./mach test-wpt --release --timeout-multiplier 2` on Linux with
GStreamer of html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements and
the-video-element, html/canvas/element/manual/drawing-images-to-the-canvas
and imagebitmap, fetch/range, content-security-policy/media-src,
html/browsers/browsing-the-web/read-media and
mozilla/media-autoplay-seek-after-ended.html with the series, 507 tests:
the unexpected results also occur with the phase 1 build of ohos-main
(load-removes-queued-error-event hangs, media_fragment_seek fails,
track-remove-by-setting-innerHTML timed out 1 of 6 isolated runs here
and 2 of 6 there, autoplay-default-permissions-policy passes where a
timeout is expected); loop-from-ended failed 1 of 26 isolated runs here
and 1 of 26 there. `./mach check` and `./mach check --ohos` pass.

AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented and tested by the agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
